  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">SUPPORT TO TANZANIA WILDLIFE MANAGEMENT AUTHORITY IN THE PROTECTION OF AFRICAN ELEPHANTS</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">THE PURPOSE OF THIS PROJECT IS TO MAINTAIN ANTI-POACHING PRESSURE IN A SEVERELY DEPLETED ELEPHANT POPULATION IN CENTRAL TANZANIA. ELEPHANTS IN THE SELOUS ECOSYSTEM WERE ESTIMATED AT OVER 100,000 INDIVIDUALS IN 1976, DECLINING BY 85 IN 2014 TO APPROXIMATELY 15,000 ANIMALS. THE MOST RECENT WAVE OF POACHING PRESSURE PEAKED BETWEEN 2010 AND 2013 WITH AN ESTIMATED SIX ELEPHANTS KILLED EACH DAY. POACHING HAS SINCE STABILIZED TO ABOUT TWO ELEPHANTS KILLED EVERY MONTH IN THE SELOUS GAME RESERVE, ONE OF THE LARGEST PROTECTED AREAS IN AFRICA, WHERE RESERVE STAFF AND PATROL TEAMS ARE RESPONSIBLE FOR CONTROLLING ILLEGAL ACTIVITIES ACROSS AN AREA NEARLY THE SIZE OF WEST VIRGINIA. THIS PROJECT BUILDS ON SUSTAINED ANTI-POACHING EFFORTS TO PROVIDE ESSENTIAL SECURITY FOR ELEPHANTS AND TO WORK TOWARDS THE STABILIZATION AND RECOVERY OF ONE OF TANZANIA S LARGEST ELEPHANT POPULATIONS. SPECIFIC ACTIVITIES INCLUDE: (1) AERIAL SURVEILLANCE AND SUPPORT, INCLUDING FUEL AND MAINTENANCE, AND OPERATIONS (2) IMPROVEMENT OF COMMUNICATIONS NETWORK, INCLUDING INSTALLATION OF RADIO EQUIPMENT THAT EXPANDS THE NETWORK TO ADDITIONAL RESERVE SECTORS (3) GROUND SURVEILLANCE AND PROTECTION SUPPORT, INCLUDING TRANSPORT, RANGER OPERATIONS, AND A MOBILE TEAM TO RESPOND RAPIDLY TO ILLEGAL ACTIVITIES AND INCIDENTS OF HUMAN-WILDLIFE CONFLICT.</narrative>
  </description>
